    <body>
      <sec id="article-40441.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Ventriculostomy is a common and life-saving neurosurgical procedure used to manage acute hydrocephalus, monitor intracranial pressure, and treat complications such as shunt infections or obstructions. The technique involves placing a catheter into the cerebral ventricles to drain cerebrospinal fluid, relieving elevated intracranial pressure and preventing further neurologic compromise. Advances in image-guided and neuronavigation-assisted techniques have improved accuracy and safety, yet ventriculostomy remains associated with significant risks, including ventriculitis, hemorrhage, malposition, obstruction, and overdrainage. Clinical success depends on precise catheter placement, adherence to aseptic technique, and vigilant postoperative monitoring. Despite procedural standardization, outcome variability persists due to inconsistent protocol adherence, inadequate infection prevention measures, and differences in interprofessional communication. Effective management requires a multidisciplinary approach involving neurosurgeons, critical care clinicians, nurses, and infection prevention specialists to ensure patient safety and optimize neurological recovery.</p>
        <p>This course teaches clinicians comprehensive knowledge of ventriculostomy indications, contraindications, procedural techniques, and evidence-based management strategies. Learners develop competence in selecting appropriate entry sites, applying neuronavigation tools, and implementing standardized care bundles to reduce complications. Emphasis is placed on sterile technique, consistent intracranial pressure monitoring, and prompt recognition of catheter-related issues. The course reinforces the value of interprofessional collaboration, as coordinated efforts among neurosurgery, nursing, infectious disease, and critical care teams enhance early detection of complications, improve communication, and support timely interventions. By integrating evidence-based protocols and collaborative care practices, clinicians strengthen procedural safety, reduce morbidity, and advance neurologic outcomes for patients requiring ventriculostomy.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the indications, contraindications, and anatomical landmarks for ventriculostomy, and describe how interprofessional collaboration enhances procedural safety.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate the role of sterile technique, evidence-based checklists, and care bundle strategies in reducing ventriculostomy-related complications.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Identify potential complications of the ventriculostomy procedure and strategies to minimize them.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement monitoring protocols and communication strategies with nurses, neurosurgeons, and anesthesiologists to promptly recognize and respond to complications, such as infection, malposition, or overdrainage.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=40441&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=40441">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-40441.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Ventriculostomy is one of the most common emergency neurosurgical procedures performed worldwide.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r1">[1]</xref> Approximately 20,000 patients undergo ventriculostomy annually in the United States alone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Claude-Nicholas Le Cat is credited with performing the first successful ventricular puncture, although his initial attempts were unsuccessful. In the 1700s, Robert Whytt and Benjamin Hill believed the procedure would hasten death, so they considered it a last resort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>In 1941, Ingraham and Campbell were successful in performing the procedure using the following innovations:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>A closed drainage system</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A stopcock&#x000a0;to prevent overdrainage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A drip chamber to prevent backflow of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A flexible silicone tube for subcutaneous tunneling</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>This success helped to reestablish the use of the procedure internationally. In 1948, Franklin Robinson coupled ventricular pressure monitoring with the drainage procedure. Intracranial pressure (ICP)-guided rescue therapy for severe&#x000a0;traumatic brain injury&#x000a0;was subsequently recommended by the Brain Trauma Foundation guidelines in 1995.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-40441.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>Herophilus and Erasistratus were the first to anatomically describe the brain's ventricular system. Later, Domenico Felice Antonio Cotugno described the presence of CSF within this interconnecting system. The ventricular system develops from cavities within&#x000a0;the embryonic brain vesicles. The cavity of the rhombencephalon forms the fourth ventricle, the cavity of the diencephalon forms the third ventricle, and the telencephalon develops into the lateral ventricles. The cavity&#x000a0;of the mesencephalon forms the cerebral aqueduct, which connects the third&#x000a0;and fourth ventricles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-40441.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>Indications for performing ventriculostomy include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Acute symptomatic hydrocephalus:</bold>&#x000a0;Most commonly following subarachnoid hemorrhage, stroke, or meningitis.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Intracranial pressure monitoring:</bold>&#x000a0;A flexible external ventricular drain protocol&#x000a0;is associated with&#x000a0;improved&#x000a0;outcomes&#x000a0;among&#x000a0;patients with severe&#x000a0;traumatic brain injury.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Infected ventriculoperitoneal shunts:&#x000a0;</bold>Adjunct management for malfunctioning shunts.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<bold>Intraoperative brain edema</bold>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Therapeutic interventions:</bold>&#x000a0;Administration of<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>thrombolytics, antibiotics,&#x000a0;or vasodilators. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/25891">"Nimodipine,"</ext-link> for further information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r8">[8]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-40441.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>The contraindications for ventriculostomy include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Concurrent anticoagulant&#x000a0;therapy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Coagulopathy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Scalp infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intracranial abscess. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23700">Intracranial Pressure Monitoring,</ext-link>" for further information.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-40441.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>Basic equipment sets&#x000a0;for ventriculostomy include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Preparation:</bold> Nonsterile gloves, soap, brush, hand towel, razor, and a marker pen for placement of monitoring devices.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Procedure:</bold>&#x000a0;A face mask, sterile gown and gloves, antiseptic solution, drape, local anesthetic agent, 5-mL syringe, 11- or 15-gauge surgical blade, ventricular catheter, and ICP-monitoring kit. Depending on the technique, a drill with a drill bit, a bolt, an ICP sensor, and a transducer may be required. Suture&#x000a0;material and sterile dressing are also needed.&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23700">Intracranial Pressure Monitoring,</ext-link>" for further information.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-40441.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>The healthcare team typically includes the following members:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Neurosurgeon</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Qualified surgical assistant</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Attending nurse</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anesthesiologist</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>General duty assistant</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23700">Intracranial Pressure Monitoring,</ext-link>" for further information.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-40441.s8" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>Patients and caregivers should receive a&#x000a0;detailed explanation of the procedure and its associated risks, and written informed consent should be obtained before the procedure.&#x000a0;Strict adherence to aseptic guidelines is a cornerstone of preventing infection. Prophylactic antibiotics should be administered&#x000a0;at the initiation of the procedure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;A meticulous technique is pivotal in minimizing procedure-related complications. Patients should be adequately sedated, have a secured airway, and receive a local anesthetic at the designated ventriculostomy site.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-40441.s9" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>The placement of a burr hole or the use of a twist drill technique&#x000a0;facilitates insertion of the device. Accurate marking of the catheter insertion site is imperative.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Points of ventricular puncture include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Keen point:</bold> 2.5 to 3 cm posterior and 2.5 to 3 cm superior to the pinna of the ear</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Kocher point:</bold>&#x000a0;1 to 2 cm anterior to the coronal suture in the midpupillary line, or 11 cm posterior to the glabella and 3 to 4 cm lateral to the midline, ipsilateral to the medial canthus, along a line extending coronally from the ipsilateral tragus
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>In freehand ventriculostomy from this point, the contralateral medial canthus and the region perpendicular to the skull are more reliable surface landmarks for accurate ventriculostomy than the ipsilateral medial canthus. Kocher point is the preferred location for ventriculostomy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r11">[11]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Dandy point:</bold> 3 cm superior and 2 cm lateral to the inion</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Frazier point:</bold> 6 cm superior and 4 cm lateral to the inion</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Paine point:</bold>&#x000a0;The apex of an isosceles triangle with a base of 3.5 cm along the Sylvian veins and sides of&#x000a0;2.5 cm</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Tubbs point:</bold>&#x000a0;Medial to the midpapillary point, through the roof of the orbit
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The trajectory angles 45&#x000ba; from a horizontal line and 15&#x000b0; to 20&#x000ba; medial to the vertical line.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r4">[4]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Ghajar first introduced a ventricular catheter guide to optimize the trajectory during ventriculostomy. He advocated a perpendicular trajectory relative to the surface of the skull.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;However, the calvarial slope&#x000a0;often limits this trajectory, especially in the coronal plane.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;The accuracy of the ideal placement of the ventricular tip is approximately 86%. Furthermore, 3.1% of the catheters&#x000a0;are nonfunctional and require replacement or repositioning.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;The accuracy improves with ultrasonography, endoscopy, neuronavigation, and the adjustable Ghajar guide technique.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Kakarla Grading of Catheter Placement:</bold>
<bold>
</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Grade 1</bold> (optimal): Ipsilateral frontal horn, including the tip of the third ventricle</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Grade 2</bold> (suboptimal, noneloquent area): Contralateral frontal horn, corpus callosum, or interhemispheric fissure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Grade 3</bold> (suboptimal, eloquent area): Basal ganglia, internal capsule, thalamus, brainstem, cerebellum, basal cisterns, and occipital cortex&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r16">[16]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-40441.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>The complications of ventriculostomy include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Intracranial and tract hemorrhage</bold> (10%): Usually clinically insignificant and rarely requires surgical intervention. The use of an antiplatelet agent within 96 hours of insertion is a significant risk&#x000a0;factor&#x000a0;for postprocedural hemorrhage (odds ratio, 13.1).</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ventriculitis</bold> (20%): Infection is the most common complication of external ventricular drainage (EVD), with rates ranging from 0% to 45%.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Technical failure or misplacement</bold> (5%): A study's results revealed that 22.4% of catheter tips were located in extraventricular spaces during freehand placement, indicating&#x000a0;the need for improved accuracy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Malpositioned tips are associated with an increased risk of shunt occlusion.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cost:</bold>&#x000a0;Placing an EVD costs approximately $200, with transducers adding $400 to $600.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Overdrainage</bold>: This complication can lead to aneurysmal rebleed and, in hydrocephalus, superior&#x000a0;transtentorial herniation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Ventriculostomy tube blockage</bold>: Obstruction by air, blood, and debris is common, leading to false ICP recordings.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<bold>Vascular injury</bold>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<bold>Pneumocephalus and pneumoventriculi</bold>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<bold>CSF leak</bold>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<bold>Catheter fracture</bold>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<bold>Dislodgement of the catheter</bold>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Obstruction by choroid plexus, blood clot, or proteinous plug:</bold> The anterior&#x000a0;entry site has a lower risk of shunt failure compared with the posterior entry by approximately one-third due to the "chandelier effect."<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;Overdrainage can lead to catheter obstruction due to the formation of ependymal bands.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Results from a study&#x000a0;evaluated outcomes among patients with primary and secondary intraventricular hemorrhages showed that approximately 40% developed complications&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;35% required permanent CSF diversion procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Factors that&#x000a0;increase the risk of infection include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<bold>CSF leak</bold>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<bold>System obstruction</bold>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Frequent CSF sampling</bold> (each sampling increases the risk by 8.3%)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Increased duration of catheter placement</bold> (9.3% with &#x02265;8 days)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Frequent catheter changes</bold> (each change increases risk 4-fold)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<bold>Catheter irrigation&#x000a0;</bold>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r2">[2]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r22">[22]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r23">[23]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r24">[24]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r25">[25]</xref>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Skin microbiota and biofilm play a crucial role in the pathogenesis of infection. However, a consensus definition of ventriculostomy-related infections (VRI) has not been established.&#x000a0;Although the incidence of VRI can be as high as 35%, as many as 25% of patients&#x000a0;receive&#x000a0;unnecessary broad-spectrum antibiotics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;Notably, the cumulative rates for definitive VRI are&#x000a0;nearly half those of suspected VRI (8.4% vs 13.5%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;No single intervention successfully prevents infection; however, limiting the duration of ventricular catheterization is the most effective.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Furthermore, a stewardship and ventriculostomy-related infection score may guide antibiotic decision-making.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;No significant differences in infection risk or complication have been observed&#x000a0;between&#x000a0;EVDs placed in the operating room versus at the bedside. The surgeons' experience also did not influence the outcome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r30">[30]</xref> An absolute risk reduction of&#x000a0;approximately 20% has been reported when an antibiotic-coated catheter is used.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;The anatomical point of ventriculostomy has not been shown to affect&#x000a0;the risk of infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;However, strict adherence to EVD care bundles is crucial in reducing the risk of infection. The&#x000a0;best strategy is to avoid insertion&#x000a0;unless necessary.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>The misplacement rate of the EVD ranges from 12.3% to 60%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from a systematic review&#x000a0;found the ipsilateral medial epicanthus as the favored anatomical trajectory&#x000a0;for frontal ventriculostomy. Kakarla grade 1 placement was achieved in 68.58% of procedures, while Kakarla grade 3 placement occurred in 10.21% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r32">[32]</xref> Suboptimal placements were associated with longer intracranial catheter length.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;Using navigation and augmented reality improves the accurate placement of EVDs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;Using bolt EVDs has minimal risk of CSF leak and tube migration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Rapid weaning within 24 hours&#x000a0;is recommended&#x000a0;over gradual elevation and clamping of the drain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-40441.s11" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Ventricular catheters&#x000a0;provide global ICP monitoring with minimal drift and influence from pressure gradients between the parenchyma and ventricular system. EVPs are the most reliable method for achieving maximum accuracy at minimal expense. Additional therapeutic benefits of CSF drainage include the instillation of medications such as antibiotics and thrombolytic agents. The advantage of the ventricular monitoring device is its ability to facilitate the egress of CSF&#x000a0;during a sustained rise in ICP (&#x02265;20 mm Hg&#x000a0;for &#x02265;5 minutes). However, the disadvantage is that simultaneous monitoring and CSF drainage are impossible. The recommended target ICP (10 mm Hg) or ICP waveform analysis guides the amount of CSF drained. This value is obtained from the concurrent application of intraparenchymal monitors or clinical neurological examination. Paradoxical upward transtentorial herniation&#x000a0;is prevented by avoiding&#x000a0;excessive drainage of CSF.</p>
        <p>An EVD can be removed once ICP normalizes and the motor score is at least 5 for 48 to 72 hours without any interventions such as clamping or gradual height increases (training of the EVD).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;Strict aseptic precautions must be implemented during removal. The head should be lowered to prevent the risk of pneumocephalus and pneumoventriculi. If a fever with meningitis-like symptoms persists, the catheter tip may be sent for bacteriological analysis. The wound is closed in layers to minimize the CSF leak and infection risk.&#x000a0;Patients&#x000a0;require close monitoring for any signs of clinical deterioration for at least 24 hours after removal, with preparations in place for the emergency reinsertion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>Substantial gaps&#x000a0;in adherence to EVD care guidelines include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>EVD clamp trials (81% adherence)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Indication-based CSF sampling (67% adherence)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tunneling catheter placement (40% adherence)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r39">[39]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Therefore, the implementation of quality improvement initiatives is significant.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-40441.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>The accuracy of the ventricular catheter&#x000a0;placement can be&#x000a0;improved through:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Three-dimensional&#x02013;printed guide models&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Real-time ultrasonography from burr hole&#x02013;compatible devices</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mobile-based augmented reality systems (iSurgARy)&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Robotic systems&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Electromagnetic navigation&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r42">[42]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>To improve&#x000a0;outcomes and prioritize patient safety, mandatory checklists should be implemented by the interprofessional team.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;Recommended guidelines include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Valid treatment order sheet, signed and dated by the surgeon</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Clearly specified reportable limits</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Education of nurses on ventriculostomy management, including the interpretation of tracing waveforms and the timely reporting of abnormalities</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>EVD drainage point at a prescribed level with the&#x000a0;transducer leveled to the tragus of the ear</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Oscillating EVD column</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitoring for a normal ICP waveform finding</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Verification of the pulsatile&#x000a0;ICP waveform on the monitor</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>No seepage at the wound site or at any junctions within the monitoring system</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Judicious assessment of drained CSF volume</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Frequent&#x000a0;evaluation of neurological status&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Strict maintenance of an aseptic environment</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Monitoring ICP requires an interprofessional team approach involving clinicians, specialists, and specialty-trained nurses collaborating across disciplines to achieve optimal patient outcomes. Surgically trained nurses are crucial in assisting and monitoring the surgeon intraoperatively. Deviation from these standards can result in life-threatening complications. Open communication&#x000a0;among team members ensures optimal outcomes and patient safety.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-40441.s13" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions</title>
        <p>Members of the interprofessional healthcare team assist in the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Drawing CSF for culture</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Assisting the surgeon&#x000a0;with&#x000a0;medication administration</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitoring for signs of intracranial hypertension</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Checking for CSF leak</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ensuring aseptic technique when manipulating the catheter</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Monitoring the ICP tracing for dampening or lack of pulsation</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-40441.s14" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Monitoring">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Monitoring</title>
        <p>Nurses involved in patient care should monitor the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hourly CSF drainage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Oscillation of CSF within the tubes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Absence of&#x000a0;wound seepage</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Correct height of the EVD</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Zeroing of the EVD height at the level of the foramen of Monro or tragus of the ear</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Frequent neurological assessments&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r8">[8]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Strict adherence to recommended checklists is required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Maintaining a sterile environment and implementing stringent monitoring to&#x000a0;anticipate and promptly address complications are the cornerstones of the care bundle approach.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-40441.r43">[43]</xref></p>
      </sec>
